  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 255 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nThe upper level high shifts over the Southern Plains on Sunday, \nbringing slight ridging across the Central Plains. Meanwhile, an \nupper level low tracks across southern British Columbia and southern \nAlberta, tracking a trough across the Intermountain West. As this \npattern evolves, expecting a jet max to develop across portions of \nWyoming and possibly into South Dakota. As this occurs, do expect \nsome stronger winds aloft across most of western and north central \nNebraska, with forecast winds speeds at 500 mb around 40 to 45 \nknots, bringing some deep layer shear across the region. However, \nthe overnight convection from Saturday night and subsequent passing \nof the surface boundary may limit the return of moisture across \nportions of western Nebraska, specifically areas west of Highway 83, \nwhere dewpoints may only rebound into the upper 50s. Highs are still \nexpected to remain quite warm on Sunday as well, with highs climbing \ninto the 90s across most of the region. Unsurprisingly, this leads \nto a fairly dry lower layer of the environment, and forecast \nsoundings are strongly indicating this to continue through the \nafternoon and evening. With this in mind, if showers and \nthunderstorms are able to develop late Sunday afternoon into the \nevening, expecting these will mostly be high based storms, offering \nlittle, if any rain. Also, given relatively sparse amounts of \ninstability aloft, not expecting a strong severe threat. However, \ngiven the strong mixing and potential for inverted-V soundings to \nbecome a reality, still cannot completely rule out an isolated \nthreat for damaging wind gusts with the strongest storms. For this \nreason, the Storm Prediction Center maintains a Marginal Risk (Level \n1 of 5) for most of the region on Sunday, with the main threat \nremaining damaging wind gust potential.\n\nMonday and Tuesday, the upper level low continues tracking east from \nsouthern Alberta to the Great Lakes region. As this low tracks east, \na few shortwaves are expected to track across the region, bringing \npotential for showers and thunderstorms both days. Precipitation \nchances appear more likely on Monday evening into the nighttime \nhours, as the upper shortwave tracks a jet max across the region. At \nthis time, the severe weather risk appears to be less certain. \nHowever, guidance does continue to suggest areas of higher \ninstability and deep layer shear across the region, which may still \nsupport strong to severe thunderstorms. There is also strong deep \nlayer shear expected on Tuesday, however the available instability \nis a bit more in question. While the exact severe risk is still a \nlittle uncertain, will continue to keep an eye on forecast trends \nfor early next week, as precipitation looks likely at least through \nMonday night, with another round possible into Tuesday night. \n\nWednesday onward, upper level ridging amplifies a bit more, favoring \na warmer, drier forecast through the end of the week. As of now, \nprecipitation chances remain generally less than 20 percent each \nevening. Ensemble guidance paints a similar picture, with chances of \nmeasureable precipitation around 20 to 30 percent, with chances of \none tenth of an inch or greater generally less than 20 percent. So, \neven if there is some potential for precipitation late week, \nexpecting it would be very light. The upper level ridging will also \nsupport warmer than average temperatures through the end of the \nweek. In fact, current highs are forecast to be in the upper 80s to \nmid 90s. For reference, typical highs to start off September are \nright around 85 degrees across the region.",
